WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.381
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.381 --> 00:00:09.862
The first people to spot the Russian
invasion of Ukraine were actually on

00:00:09.862 --> 00:00:11.630
the other side of the globe.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:17.782
Before Putin had ever said anything
about his assault a group of academics

00:00:17.782 --> 00:00:22.321
in California noticed an unusual
buildup of traffic at the border and

00:00:22.321 --> 00:00:25.700
concluded correctly that
the invasion had begun.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.500
How did they do it?

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.849
Using satellite technology.

00:00:32.379 --> 00:00:35.979
Six months ago I left active
duty with the Marine Corps and

00:00:35.979 --> 00:00:39.510
joined a defense technology
startup in Washington DC.

00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:46.070
I was surprised when they asked me to
help build a military space strategy.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:49.580
I didn't know anything about space.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:53.520
I could barely tell you the difference
between star wars and star Trek.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:59.616
I did want to be an astronaut as a kid but
if you're afraid of

00:00:59.616 --> 00:01:04.600
roller coasters you're probably
scared of rocket ships too.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.558
I did learn that summer that for
the US space is considered the ultimate

00:01:09.558 --> 00:01:14.530
high ground and as a result we've
already militarized it quite a bit.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.903
Above us right now there are enough
satellites to take a picture

00:01:21.903 --> 00:01:26.470
of every square meter of
the planet multiple times a day.

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:33.000
In 2023 the US is planning to field
a space based laser that's next year.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:41.010
But guess who else is militarizing
space Russia and China.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:45.059
As a Marine officer I can tell
you we're in a dead sprint and

00:01:45.059 --> 00:01:47.300
no one is settling for a second.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:53.779
But here's what concerns me
using space to find better

00:01:53.779 --> 00:01:56.584
ways to kill each other is easy.

00:01:56.584 --> 00:02:04.052
What's harder is using space
constructively to lift all boats and

00:02:04.052 --> 00:02:08.860
not just deep in great power competition.

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:11.885
Space is supposed to be
the final frontier so

00:02:11.885 --> 00:02:15.825
is militarizing the hell out
of it the best that we can do?

00:02:17.988 --> 00:02:22.540
Maybe there's a better way this
past quarter I took a class called

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:24.660
Climate Ventures.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.156
Where I teamed up with aeronautics and

00:02:27.156 --> 00:02:33.011
astronautics students to explore ways that
space technology can solve climate change.

00:02:33.011 --> 00:02:38.130
During our research I found this really
interesting company called GHG Set.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:43.655
They're using satellite based
radar technology to visually

00:02:43.655 --> 00:02:47.460
identify methane emissions
across the planet.

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:49.426
The first time we've ever
been able to do that.

00:02:49.426 --> 00:02:55.489
Another company another approach
sponsored by Harvard University and

00:02:55.489 --> 00:03:00.070
backed by Bill Gates uses
a different approach.

00:03:00.070 --> 00:03:05.738
They use space technology to spray fine
particles into the upper atmosphere and

00:03:05.738 --> 00:03:10.510
scatter sunlight and
actually cause global cooling.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:13.786
That would be a game changer for
global warming but

00:03:13.786 --> 00:03:16.130
climate change is just an example.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:21.917
I think that space technology
has the ability to solve many

00:03:21.917 --> 00:03:27.510
problems in many industries for
the benefit of all of us.

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:32.910
And it would be great if commercial
space was on par with military space.

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:41.220
But there's two problems with that first
those space is technically infinite.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:46.787
We're actually running out of it really
fast between 90 to 600 miles above

00:03:46.787 --> 00:03:52.760
the planet is a sweet spot where 90%
of satellites have been deployed.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:55.960
We call this low earth orbit or

00:03:55.960 --> 00:04:01.470
LEO 90 to 600 miles sounds like a lot.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:07.290
But in LEO satellites don't sit
still they actually move and orbit.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.123
So instead of picturing a dot
that surrounds the planet you

00:04:13.123 --> 00:04:17.803
should actually picture a ring
that goes around it instead.

00:04:19.687 --> 00:04:24.650
In 2021 the UN estimated that
there were 7500 satellites in LEO.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:31.458
That's a lot of rings it creates a real
traffic jam on this case a space Jam.

00:04:34.558 --> 00:04:39.641
What makes it more complicated is that
when satellites collide with each other or

00:04:39.641 --> 00:04:43.986
when they reach the end of their
useful life they actually get stuck.

00:04:43.986 --> 00:04:47.140
Because we don't yet
know how to remove space debris.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:52.585
So when broken satellites and
debris are in space and

00:04:52.585 --> 00:04:57.036
LEO they create a higher likelihood for
more accidents.

00:04:57.036 --> 00:05:01.025
Which creates more debris and
it's a vicious cycle.

00:05:01.025 --> 00:05:08.380
It's hard to tell how much space is left
in LEO but we're definitely running out.

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:11.122
This leads to the second problem.

00:05:11.122 --> 00:05:16.447
Because space technology is so
expensive defense has an outsized

00:05:16.447 --> 00:05:21.010
voice in how commercial space
dollars are being spent.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:26.116
Sure SpaceX is $100 billion company but

00:05:26.116 --> 00:05:31.017
when the government is spending
$54 billion on space per

00:05:31.017 --> 00:05:35.440
year it's hard to ignore
them in your business case.

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:41.786
Military organizations like Space Force
and the National Reconnaissance Office

00:05:41.786 --> 00:05:46.070
are also outsourcing their space
technology to the commercial sector.

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:52.161
So even though it looks like a lot of
money is flowing into the commercial

00:05:52.161 --> 00:05:56.186
sector into commercial space it's
often just a Trojan horse for

00:05:56.186 --> 00:05:57.838
more military spending.

00:06:01.141 --> 00:06:06.990
Personally I completely understand why
the US is rushing to militarize space.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:10.350
And if I'm being honest I understand why
Russia and China are doing the same.

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:15.150
But great power competition
doesn't benefit humanity.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:21.053
What benefits humanity in space
is combating climate change or

00:06:21.053 --> 00:06:23.650
frankly any use of commercial space.

00:06:24.770 --> 00:06:27.186
So I'm here to sound the alarm.

00:06:27.186 --> 00:06:31.326
The world's militaries
are filling space up fast and

00:06:31.326 --> 00:06:37.316
the opportunity to shift the trajectory
of the space industry is shrinking.

00:06:39.099 --> 00:06:40.160
So what now?

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.063
If you're someone whose relationship
with space is primarily just watching

00:06:46.063 --> 00:06:50.270
Guardians of the Galaxy you still
have a very important role to play.

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:56.696
We need to demonstrate both
an awareness of and a demand for

00:06:56.696 --> 00:07:00.000
commercial uses of space technology.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:05.595
And not just for
climate change but also for

00:07:05.595 --> 00:07:13.190
agriculture finance Urban studies
maritime activity and even politics.

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:17.013
Space technology has come so far but

00:07:17.013 --> 00:07:21.920
unless you're in the military you
probably don't know about it.

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:27.495
But now that you all do you can
help amplify the demand for

00:07:27.495 --> 00:07:29.800
commercial space technology.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:36.320
Start by exploring the imagery that's
been collected by satellite companies.

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:42.329
Any Stanford student has access to
the historical database of planet.

00:07:42.329 --> 00:07:48.444
One of the largest space companies
with nearly 500 satellites in

00:07:48.444 --> 00:07:53.495
Low Earth Orbit and LEO and
data going back to 1972.

00:07:53.495 --> 00:07:56.461
I challenge you all to use this data and

00:07:56.461 --> 00:08:02.310
show us something constructive that
you can build with space technology.

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:10.060
Planets historical database is available
to MIT and ASU students as well.

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:14.890
But I urge planet and all satellite
imagery companies to make their data

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:17.640
available to everyone and open source.

00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:22.825
Imagine the scientific doors that
we could collectively unlock.

00:08:24.787 --> 00:08:29.656
By demonstrating a groundswell
of demand and finding nobel

00:08:29.656 --> 00:08:35.620
ways to use space technology that
no one has ever seen before.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:40.443
You can help ensure that the future
direction of the space industry is

00:08:40.443 --> 00:08:45.201
equally influenced by commercial
needs as well as military needs.

00:08:45.201 --> 00:08:48.671
And avoid the over
militarization of space.

00:08:48.671 --> 00:08:53.453
I'll leave you with one last
story two days ago Russia

00:08:53.453 --> 00:08:56.541
severed the Internet in Ukraine.

00:08:56.541 --> 00:09:02.330
A classic military move to disable
your opponent's command and control.

00:09:02.330 --> 00:09:07.352
It looked like a crippling blow but
within 24 hours Elon Musk

00:09:07.352 --> 00:09:12.279
announced that Starlink
a satellite based Internet service

00:09:12.279 --> 00:09:17.410
provider would now be online in
the Ukraine for the first time.

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:23.270
Picture that space not
just an extension of our

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:28.096
earthly conflicts but
instead a solution for them.

00:09:28.096 --> 00:09:36.437
A way to resolve what we're doing to each
other here that is the power of space.

00:09:36.437 --> 00:09:37.482
Thank you.

00:09:37.482 --> 00:09:40.161
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:40.161 --> 00:09:42.472
[MUSIC]

